We compare joint generation against post-hoc BM25 retrieval (entity-constrained, $k{=}2$), using Evi-F1 (joint) with the DREEAM-protocol denominator~\citep{ma2023dreeam}. Post-hoc retrieval achieves higher Rel-F1 (48.76) by avoiding the evidence tax but lower Evi-F1 (joint) of 34.7 (EDCR 0.766). RSFT CED reaches 36.1 (EDCR 0.6487), a 1.4-point evidence advantage. CED reranking at inference (best-of-$N{=}8$ by CED score) achieves Rel-F1 48.64 and Evi-F1 81.88 (TP-only), approaching no-evidence extraction quality while preserving evidence.


\subsection{Generalization}
\label{sec:generalization}

Cross-architecture validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}) shows the largest RSFT gain (+5.45pp) with complete pairwise seed separation (\S\ref{sec:scaling}). The RSFT improvement transfers across datasets: on Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred}, RSFT achieves 41.45 Ign-F1 (+4.86pp over SFT 36.59), matching the DocRED gain (+5.08pp). Our best joint Evi-F1 (36.1, DREEAM-protocol) falls below supervised DREEAM (54.15\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), a gap driven by extraction performance rather than evidence quality (TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%). On the Re-DocRED test set, RSFT Ign-F1 drops approximately 20pp relative to dev across all evaluated scales (e.g., 14B: 58.5$\to$38.5), attributable to 2.82$\times$ higher annotation density in test (34.7 vs.\ 12.3 gold triples per document); model ranking is perfectly preserved (Spearman $\rho{=}1.0$; Table~\ref{tab:test_results} in Appendix). Full results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:generalization_full}.


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_evidence_tax.pdf}
\vspace{-4mm}
\caption{Inference-time evidence tax across model scales (RSFT models, Re-DocRED test). Negative values indicate evidence \emph{benefits} extraction. Error bars show $\pm$1 std (all scales have $\geq$2 seeds).}
\label{fig:evidence_tax}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

EDCR (\S\ref{sec:edcr}) validates as a diagnostic at both cross-method ($\rho = -0.96$, $n = 7$ unique methods, $p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{sec:edcr_per_relation}) and per-relation levels ($\rho = -0.79$, $n = 76$, $p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{sec:edcr_per_relation}), the latter orthogonal to Evi-F1 ($\rho = -0.06$); partial correlations controlling for shared components are virtually unchanged ($\rho = -0.9617$ vs.\ $-0.9643$). CED and flat-NLI produce comparable RSFT results (Rel-F1 45.63 vs.\ 45.61); stricter selection ($k{=}1$) outperforms $k{=}3$ by 1.02 Ign-F1 despite fewer examples (Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}). RSFT reduces truncation FN, hallucinated FP, and duplicates by 6--22\% across scales, but evidence quality is unchanged ($\Delta < 0.4$pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:error_analysis}): RSFT improves \emph{what} the model extracts but not \emph{how well} it grounds extractions---concrete evidence citation comparisons in Appendix~\ref{sec:qualitative} illustrate this pattern. Inference overhead analysis (Appendix~\ref{sec:inference_efficiency}) reveals that evidence generation adds 16--24\% wall-clock time at 4B--14B scale but paradoxically reduces inference time at 1.7B by 13.6\%, as the evidence field constrains output structure and reduces prediction count by 1.75$\times$.

\section*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Data and architecture scope.}
We evaluate on two datasets (DocRED, Re-DocRED), both derived from Wikipedia; cross-domain evaluation is constrained by the absence of evidence-annotated DocRE datasets beyond Wikipedia, a limitation shared by all prior evidence-aware work (SAIS, DREEAM, EIDER).
Evidence tax patterns span two model families (Qwen3, LLaMA) with single hyperparameter settings per RL method; other architectures, scales beyond 14B, or alternative KL coefficients may produce different decoupling behaviors. The decoupling spectrum analysis is conducted entirely at 4B scale; whether the same ordering (GRPO catastrophic $\to$ DPO gradual $\to$ RSFT none) holds at other scales remains unverified.

\paragraph{CED scoring sensitivity.}
CED scoring operates at the document level using an external NLI model (DeBERTa-v3); sensitivity to other NLI model families is unexplored, and sentence-level CED filtering disrupts co-reference chains (Evi-F1 $-$7.7pp).

\paragraph{RSFT mechanistic explanation.}
Our explanation for why RSFT avoids decoupling (that discrete selection cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off) is structural rather than empirical. We have not isolated whether RSFT's advantage stems primarily from removing low-quality training examples, from implicit data augmentation through candidate diversity, or from both. Disentangling these factors would require controlled experiments with fixed training data quality but varied selection mechanisms.

\begin{abstract}
Large Language Model (LLM)-based Document-level Relation Extraction (DocRE) now matches or exceeds encoder baselines in extraction accuracy, yet outputs bare relation triplets without identifying supporting sentences. Encoder-based evidence methods (SAIS, DREEAM) provide evidence but trail LLM approaches in accuracy, creating a gap between extraction performance and verifiability. We study evidence-grounded DocRE as structured generation and compare Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), and Rejection Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT) across three fully evaluated scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B) with 14B validation, two model families, and two datasets. The evidence tax (extraction cost of generating evidence) is scale-dependent: beneficial at 1.7B ($-$5.63pp dev Ign-F1) but approximately neutral at $\geq$4B (+0.25$\pm$0.47pp at 4B; positive $=$ cost). Gradient-based optimization amplifies evidence-extraction competition (GRPO catastrophic, DPO gradual), while RSFT with NLI-based scoring avoids decoupling through output filtering rather than gradient pressure. We define the Evidence-Decoupling Ratio (EDCR) to quantify extraction-evidence misalignment, validated at per-relation level ($\rho{=}{-}0.79$, $n{=}76$, $p{<}0.001$). Our 1.7B RSFT model (51.19 Ign-F1) approaches 8B Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) performance (50.19) at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters. Test-set evaluation preserves model ranking across all scales (Spearman $\rho{=}1.0$)\footnote{Code and data are available at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/faith-evidence-ie-18BD/}.}.
\end{abstract}

